# Findings the secret-scan gate has already been shown to be wrong about.
#
# Each line is a gitleaks *fingerprint* — `commit:file:rule-id:line`. That is the narrowest
# exemption this tool offers, and it is deliberately the one used here: a fingerprint spares one
# known string at one line of one commit under one rule. A genuinely new credential in the same
# file, on a different line, or under a different rule is still a finding. Nothing here switches
# scanning off for a path, a rule, or a directory — see the argument in `.gitleaks.toml`, which
# holds path exemptions to a high bar and is unchanged by this file.
#
# ## Why these seven exist
#
# All seven are test fixtures, and the gate has failed on them on *every* run since they were
# written — including the nightly that filed #9 as a credential-in-history finding. They cannot
# clear themselves: the commits are immutable, so without this file the gate is structurally
# incapable of ever going green.
#
# Two were read at the flagged commit rather than trusted by filename:
#
#   * `tools/eslint-plugin-compass/tests/rules.test.js:251` assigns a twelve-character alphanumeric
#     literal to an `apiToken` key, inside a case asserting that `no-credential-literal` FLAGS a
#     hardcoded token. The fixture has to look like a token or the test asserts nothing. The value
#     is deliberately NOT reproduced here — see "One more thing this file learned" at the bottom.
#     Note `.gitleaks.toml` exempts the plugin's
#     `rules/` directory and deliberately not its `tests/`, which is why this line is here and not
#     covered there — and that narrowness is correct, so it is preserved rather than widened.
#
#   * `apps/web/tests/error-reporting.test.ts:33` is an `sk_live_`-prefixed string whose body is a
#     hand-written descending-hex pattern repeated twice, beside a JWT signed
#     `abcdefghijklmnopqrstuvwxyz012345` and an `@northwind.example` address. Synthetic on every
#     axis except the prefix — and the prefix is the whole reason `stripe-access-token` fired.
#
# The remaining five are the `ghp_`/`ghr_` token-handling fixtures in `token-storage.test.ts` and
# `integration-tokens.test.ts`, at commits predating their reshaping.
#
# ## The part worth keeping
#
# At HEAD these fixtures read `ghp_EXAMPLE-FIXTURE-NOT-A-REAL-CREDENTIAL`, and
# `packages/auth/tests/integration-tokens.test.ts` explains why: a fixture written in a live
# token's shape "is indistinguishable from a real leaked credential to every reader and every
# scanner … so it costs a reviewer real time to rule out and trains everyone to wave through the
# pattern that matters." That reasoning is right, and this file is the bill for the period before
# it was applied. Keep writing fixtures that way and this list never needs another entry.
#
# ## The standard for adding a line here
#
# Read the value at the flagged commit and satisfy yourself it is not a credential. Do not add a
# fingerprint to make a build green — a real leak and a fixture look identical in the report, which
# is the entire reason this file demands the commit be opened. If a value turns out to be real, the
# fix is rotation, not an entry here.
#
# ## One more thing this file learned, the hard way
#
# The first version of these comments quoted the flagged fixture value verbatim to justify sparing
# it. The gate then reported `.gitleaksignore:19` at entropy 3.584963 — the same entropy as the
# finding it was explaining. Seven findings became one, and the one was this file.
#
# Deleting the line at HEAD fixed nothing, and that is the part worth internalising: the gate scans
# **history**, so the commit carrying the bad line still reported it. The finding was only removable
# because the branch was unmerged and could be squashed — a commit is editable for exactly as long
# as it has not reached `master`, and not one moment longer.
#
# The alternative would have been adding a fingerprint for the offending commit, which *would* have
# worked (a historical commit's fingerprint is stable). It was the wrong fix: it permanently exempts
# a mistake that could still be erased, and every such entry makes this list a worse signal.
#
# `.gitleaks.toml` already warned about exactly this shape — the ESLint `rules/` directory "reports
# itself" because it describes credential shapes for a living — and this file walked into it anyway.
#
# So: **describe flagged values, never reproduce them.** "A twelve-character alphanumeric literal
# assigned to `apiToken`" carries the whole argument and matches no rule. A prefix on its own
# (`sk_live_`, `ghp_`) is safe to name, because a prefix is not a secret and naming it is often the
# only way to say which rule fired.

